WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.870
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll use

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.857
serialization and deserialization to save and

00:00:06.857 --> 00:00:09.711
restore object state, then check a restored

00:00:09.711 --> 00:00:11.636
value's type before using it.

00:00:12.292 --> 00:00:15.297
These skills let applications preserve settings

00:00:15.297 --> 00:00:18.110
and records between runs while checking that

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:20.731
recovered values are the types their code

00:00:20.731 --> 00:00:21.242
expects.

00:00:21.889 --> 00:00:24.934
A campus equipment desk keeps the location label

00:00:24.934 --> 00:00:26.647
lab in a small card object.

00:00:26.647 --> 00:00:29.503
We will save that card to a private temporary

00:00:29.503 --> 00:00:31.216
file and restore its label.

00:00:31.216 --> 00:00:33.880
The reader must check that it received the

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.672
expected card before calling the method that

00:00:36.672 --> 00:00:37.814
returns the label.

00:00:38.459 --> 00:00:41.466
Our report should show Label, followed by lab,

00:00:41.466 --> 00:00:44.343
confirming that the desk recovered its saved

00:00:44.343 --> 00:00:44.931
location.

00:00:44.931 --> 00:00:47.938
Then we'll change the stored value and predict

00:00:47.938 --> 00:00:49.834
how the same reader responds.

00:00:50.493 --> 00:00:53.022
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:00:53.022 --> 00:00:55.493
Main.java and build the card's round trip.

00:00:59.283 --> 00:01:02.234
We need object input and output streams, the

00:01:02.234 --> 00:01:05.452
serialization marker, and the familiar Files and

00:01:05.452 --> 00:01:06.190
Path types.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:09.007
Add these imports so their short names are

00:01:09.007 --> 00:01:10.683
available in the program.

00:01:23.722 --> 00:01:26.724
Next, define the card with its String label, a

00:01:26.724 --> 00:01:29.727
constructor to set that field, and a getter to

00:01:29.727 --> 00:01:30.249
read it.

00:01:30.249 --> 00:01:33.251
Mark the class as supporting serialization and

00:01:33.251 --> 00:01:35.797
give it an explicit version identifier.

00:02:00.617 --> 00:02:03.283
Serializable is a marker interface: it declares

00:02:03.283 --> 00:02:05.835
support without requiring a serialize method.

00:02:05.835 --> 00:02:08.331
Our String field supports serialization too.

00:02:08.331 --> 00:02:10.826
The static version identifier belongs to the

00:02:10.826 --> 00:02:13.492
class, final prevents reassignment, and long is

00:02:13.492 --> 00:02:14.740
its whole-number type.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.235
The L suffix makes the value a long literal.

00:02:17.235 --> 00:02:19.731
Matching that identifier does not make every

00:02:19.731 --> 00:02:21.092
class change compatible.

00:02:21.114 --> 00:02:23.864
Main holds the conventional entry method for

00:02:23.864 --> 00:02:25.302
this Workspace program.

00:02:25.302 --> 00:02:28.239
We will declare the checked exceptions that may

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:31.239
leave it, create one private temporary file, and

00:02:31.239 --> 00:02:33.364
start the outer cleanup structure.

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:54.888
The throws declaration allows I/O and

00:02:54.888 --> 00:02:58.038
class-lookup failures to leave main; it does not

00:02:58.038 --> 00:02:58.826
handle them.

00:02:58.826 --> 00:03:01.976
CreateTempFile creates an empty file and returns

00:03:01.976 --> 00:03:02.567
its Path.

00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:05.586
We retain that location for both stream phases

00:03:05.586 --> 00:03:06.439
and deletion.

00:03:06.439 --> 00:03:09.393
A dot bin suffix labels the name but does not

00:03:09.393 --> 00:03:11.034
select the stored format.

00:03:11.074 --> 00:03:14.277
Open byte output and wrap it in an object output

00:03:14.277 --> 00:03:14.744
stream.

00:03:14.744 --> 00:03:17.880
Pass a new LabelCard holding lab to the writer,

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.949
then finish the resource block before reading.

00:03:40.856 --> 00:03:43.579
WriteObject receives the card and writes its

00:03:43.579 --> 00:03:45.497
supported state representation.

00:03:45.497 --> 00:03:48.406
It does not save Java source, a running method,

00:03:48.406 --> 00:03:49.829
or the notebook kernel.

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:52.613
The version identifier is class compatibility

00:03:52.613 --> 00:03:55.584
information, separate from the ordinary instance

00:03:55.584 --> 00:03:55.955
label.

00:03:55.955 --> 00:03:58.863
Closing this wrapper also closes the underlying

00:03:58.863 --> 00:03:59.606
byte output.

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:02.765
Now open byte input, wrap it in an object input

00:04:02.765 --> 00:04:05.160
stream, and restore the saved value.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:08.353
Keep the initial result in an Object variable so

00:04:08.353 --> 00:04:10.814
we can check what actually came back.

00:04:26.353 --> 00:04:29.198
ReadObject reconstructs the saved state using

00:04:29.198 --> 00:04:32.169
the compatible class available to this program.

00:04:32.169 --> 00:04:34.824
Its declared result type is Object, Java's

00:04:34.824 --> 00:04:37.226
common superclass for class instances.

00:04:37.226 --> 00:04:40.134
That reference can hold the restored card, but

00:04:40.134 --> 00:04:42.915
its declared type does not expose the card's

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.053
particular getter.

00:04:44.101 --> 00:04:47.306
Check whether the returned value is a LabelCard.

00:04:47.306 --> 00:04:50.377
In that matching branch, cast the reference to

00:04:50.377 --> 00:04:52.381
LabelCard and call its getter.

00:04:52.381 --> 00:04:54.851
Otherwise, report an unexpected type.

00:05:24.281 --> 00:05:27.037
Instanceof checks the actual value and is false

00:05:27.037 --> 00:05:27.565
for null.

00:05:27.565 --> 00:05:30.321
The cast then gives us a LabelCard reference to

00:05:30.321 --> 00:05:32.901
the same restored object; it does not create

00:05:32.901 --> 00:05:35.363
another card or convert a String into one.

00:05:35.363 --> 00:05:37.943
A cast on an incompatible non-null value can

00:05:37.943 --> 00:05:39.409
throw ClassCastException.

00:05:39.409 --> 00:05:41.989
The matching guard keeps that attempt out of

00:05:41.989 --> 00:05:43.631
this reader's mismatch path.

00:05:43.704 --> 00:05:46.662
Finish the reader block, then put deletion in

00:05:46.662 --> 00:05:48.239
the outer finally block.

00:05:48.239 --> 00:05:51.197
Closing releases the stream; deletion removes

00:05:51.197 --> 00:05:53.629
the file that this complete run owns.

00:06:05.036 --> 00:06:07.766
The reader closes before finally attempts

00:06:07.766 --> 00:06:10.630
deletion, including when the protected work

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:11.029
fails.

00:06:11.029 --> 00:06:13.693
Deletion can itself report an I/O error.

00:06:13.693 --> 00:06:16.823
With the complete supported-card path in place,

00:06:16.823 --> 00:06:19.886
let's click Run and inspect the actual report.

00:06:25.482 --> 00:06:28.014
The report shows Label, followed by lab.

00:06:28.014 --> 00:06:30.862
The restored value passed the card check, and

00:06:30.862 --> 00:06:33.077
its getter returned the saved text.

00:06:33.077 --> 00:06:35.925
This verifies the restored label; it does not

00:06:35.925 --> 00:06:38.962
mean the restored card is the original in-memory

00:06:38.962 --> 00:06:39.532
instance.

00:06:39.636 --> 00:06:42.625
For a second run, write the String lab directly

00:06:42.625 --> 00:06:44.596
instead of constructing a card.

00:06:44.596 --> 00:06:47.649
Keep the same class definition, LabelCard check,

00:06:47.649 --> 00:06:49.302
cast, and report branches.

00:06:49.302 --> 00:06:52.228
This changes the actual stored value, not what

00:06:52.228 --> 00:06:53.436
the reader expects.

00:06:59.445 --> 00:07:02.415
Before running, predict which report branch will

00:07:02.415 --> 00:07:02.910
execute.

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:05.880
What type will the restored non-null value have,

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:07.860
and will the cast or getter run?

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.768
Pause here and explain your prediction from the

00:07:10.768 --> 00:07:12.995
value actually passed to the writer.

00:07:21.104 --> 00:07:24.212
Let's click Run to test that prediction against

00:07:24.212 --> 00:07:26.129
the reader's actual behavior.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:34.635
The program reports an unexpected object type.

00:07:34.635 --> 00:07:37.572
The String was valid saved data, but it did not

00:07:37.572 --> 00:07:40.322
satisfy this reader's LabelCard requirement.

00:07:40.322 --> 00:07:42.885
The false guard skipped both the cast and

00:07:42.885 --> 00:07:43.322
getter.

00:07:43.322 --> 00:07:46.198
Declaring LabelCard elsewhere did not turn the

00:07:46.198 --> 00:07:47.885
written String into a card.

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:50.557
You saved supported state, restored it with a

00:07:50.557 --> 00:07:52.935
compatible class, and separated the Object

00:07:52.935 --> 00:07:55.595
result, runtime type check, and reference cast.

00:07:55.595 --> 00:07:58.256
Those steps let the desk recover a useful label

00:07:58.256 --> 00:08:00.407
while reporting a controlled mismatch.

00:08:00.407 --> 00:08:03.067
The notebook practice asks you to complete this

00:08:03.067 --> 00:08:05.728
agreement, repair a wrong guard, and build your

00:08:05.728 --> 00:08:06.860
own tag-card reader.

00:08:07.025 --> 00:08:09.856
For your next step, choose a small object whose

00:08:09.856 --> 00:08:11.120
state you would save.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.951
What class-specific operation should the reader

00:08:13.951 --> 00:08:15.999
use, and what must it check first?

00:08:15.999 --> 00:08:18.709
Plan both an accepted value and an unexpected

00:08:18.709 --> 00:08:21.480
type so your tests show that the reader can do

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:22.925
more than always reject.
